Name: microseconds

Synonyms: microsecond, microsec, microsecs

Type: function

Syntax: the [long] microseconds

Summary:
<return|Returns> the number of microseconds since the start of the
<eon>.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android, html5

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
put the microseconds into tStart

Example:
local tStart
put the microseconds into tStart
sort lines of field "Names"
put "Sorted in" && the microseconds - tStart && "microseconds"

Returns:
The <microseconds> <function> <return|returns> a positive <integer>.
`the long microseconds` can have a fraction, where the clock has a finer
resolution than a microsecond.

Description:
Use the <microseconds> <function> to time <event|events> that take less
than a millisecond, or to time them more finely than the <milliseconds>
can.

The <microseconds> <function> <return|returns> the total number of
microseconds since midnight GMT, January 1, 1970: the
<milliseconds> times 1000, and more finely. A microsecond is one
millionth of a second.

How finely it counts depends on the system's clock: to the microsecond
on macOS and Linux, and to a fraction of a microsecond on Windows 8 and
later (to the clock's tick, about 16 milliseconds, on Windows 7). In a
web browser, it counts in whole milliseconds.

On Windows the <milliseconds> reads the system time when the engine
starts (and again after the computer sleeps) and counts on from there,
while the <microseconds> reads the system time each time, so the two can
drift apart when the clock is set. Use one or the other to time an
<event>.

The <microseconds> can be written only with `the` before it.
<microseconds> is not a reserved word: a <variable>, a <handler>, a
parameter or a <custom property> named `microseconds` (or `microsecond`,
`microsec` or `microsecs`) works as it always has, and
`the microseconds of me` is the custom property. Written as
`microseconds()`, it is a call of a <handler>: a <function> handler of
your own with that name, if there is one, and otherwise the HyperXTalk
Compatibility library's.

A few lines meant something else before the <microseconds> was added:
`beep the microseconds` was a beep, then a call of a <handler> named
`the`, and `create button the microseconds` made a button with no
name, then called a <handler> named `microseconds`. So that scripts
work as they did, a <handler> that compiles without the <microseconds>
is read as before, every line of it, and only one that does not compile
without it is read with it; so is a <do> script or a <value>
<expression>. Any other use of `the microseconds`, such as
`put the microseconds into tStart`, did not compile before, so a
<handler> that has one is read with the <microseconds>.

The <microseconds> comes from HyperXTalk, so that stacks written for it
work unchanged.

References: function (control structure), milliseconds (function),
seconds (function), ticks (function), do (command), value (function),
event (glossary), return (glossary), eon (glossary), integer (keyword),
long (keyword), variable (glossary), handler (glossary),
custom property (glossary)

Tags: math
